### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a program that simulates a simple home automation system. The system will manage the state of various home devices and respond to user commands. Each device can be in one of two states: "on" or "off". The program should support the following commands:
- "turn on [device]" to turn on a specific device
- "turn off [device]" to turn off a specific device
- "status" to display the current state of all devices
- "exit" to terminate the program

You are given a code snippet that initializes the home automation system and defines the structure of the home controller. Your task is to implement the logic for processing user commands and updating the state of devices accordingly.

Code snippet for inspiration:
```
	/*
	|--------------------------------------------------------------------------
	| Default Home Controller
	|--------------------------------------------------------------------------
	|
	| Initialize the home automation system and define the structure of the home controller.
	|
	*/
```

Your program should support the following devices:
- Light
- TV
- Speaker
- Coffee Machine

The initial state of all devices is "off".

### Solution:
```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class HomeController {
    private static Map<String, Boolean> devices = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        initializeDevices();
        processUserCommands();
    }

    private static void initializeDevices() {
        devices.put("Light", false);
        devices.put("TV", false);
        devices.put("Speaker", false);
        devices.put("Coffee Machine", false);
    }

    private static void processUserCommands() {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        while (true) {
            System.out.print("Enter command: ");
            String input = scanner.nextLine().trim().toLowerCase();
            if (input.startsWith("turn on")) {
                String device = input.substring(8).trim();
                turnOnDevice(device);
            } else if (input.startsWith("turn off")) {
                String device = input.substring(9).trim();
                turnOffDevice(device);
            } else if (input.equals("status")) {
                displayDeviceStatus();
            } else if (input.equals("exit")) {
                break;
            } else {
                System.out.println("Invalid command. Please try again.");
            }
        }
        scanner.close();
    }

    private static void turnOnDevice(String device) {
        if (devices.containsKey(device)) {
            devices.put(device, true);
            System.out.println(device + " turned on.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Device not found.");
        }
    }

    private static void turnOffDevice(String device) {
        if (devices.containsKey(device)) {
            devices.put(device, false);
            System.out.println(device + " turned off.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Device not found.");
        }
    }

    private static void displayDeviceStatus() {
        System.out.println("Current device status:");
        for (Map.Entry<String, Boolean> entry : devices.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + (entry.getValue() ? "on" : "off"));
        }
    }
}
```

This solution provides a Java program that simulates a home automation system. It initializes the devices, processes user commands, and updates the state of devices accordingly. The program supports turning devices on or off, displaying the current device status, and exiting the system.

