#include <utility>
#include <cmath>

class SimplifyLine {
public:
    std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> simplify(const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& line) {
        // Your implementation here
    }
};
```

You need to implement the `simplify` function to remove redundant points from the input line and return the simplified line.

The unit test suite provides a starting point for testing the `SimplifyLine` class. You need to complete the implementation of the `TEST_CASE` sections to cover various scenarios and ensure the correctness of the `simplify` function.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `SimplifyLine` class and its `simplify` function and ensure that all the provided test cases pass.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <utility>
#include <cmath>

class SimplifyLine {
public:
    std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> simplify(const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& line) {
        std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> simplifiedLine;
        if (line.size() < 2) {
            return line;
        }

        simplifiedLine.push_back(line[0]);
        for (size_t i = 1; i < line.size() - 1; ++i) {
            if (!isCollinear(line[i - 1], line[i], line[i + 1])) {
                simplifiedLine.push_back(line[i]);
            }
        }
        simplifiedLine.push_back(line.back());

        return simplifiedLine;
    }

private:
    bool isCollinear(const std::pair<int, int>& p1, const std::pair<int, int>& p2, const std::pair<int, int>& p3) {
        return (p2.second - p1.second) * (p3.first - p2.first) == (p3.second - p2.second) * (p2.first - p1.first);
    }
};
```

The `simplify` function removes redundant points from the input line by checking if each point lies on the line segment between its neighboring points. If a point is found to be non-collinear with its neighbors, it is added to the simplified line. The `isCollinear` function checks if three points are collinear using the slope formula.

