You are tasked with implementing a generic rectangle class in C++ that can handle rectangles of different data types and dimensions. The provided code snippet includes the definition of two typedefs: `rect2i_t` for a rectangle with integer coordinates in 2D space, and `rect2f_t` for a rectangle with floating-point coordinates in 2D space. Your task is to create a generic `rect_tt` class template that can be instantiated with different data types and dimensions, and then implement the necessary functionality to work with these rectangles.

Your `rect_tt` class should support the following operations:
1. Construction of a rectangle with specified dimensions and data type.
2. Access and modification of the rectangle's dimensions and data type.
3. Calculation of the area of the rectangle.
4. Checking if a point lies within the rectangle.

Your implementation should be able to handle rectangles of any dimension and data type, ensuring that the operations are correctly performed regardless of the specific instantiation of the `rect_tt` class.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <array>

template <typename T, size_t N>
class rect_tt {
private:
    std::array<T, N> dimensions;

public:
    rect_tt() {
        dimensions.fill(T{});
    }

    rect_tt(const std::array<T, N>& dims) : dimensions(dims) {}

    const std::array<T, N>& getDimensions() const {
        return dimensions;
    }

    void setDimensions(const std::array<T, N>& dims) {
        dimensions = dims;
    }

    T area() const {
        T result = T{1};
        for (const auto& dim : dimensions) {
            result *= dim;
        }
        return result;
    }

    bool containsPoint(const std::array<T, N>& point) const {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
            if (point[i] < T{} || point[i] > dimensions[i]) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
};

int main() {
