You should use appropriate synchronization mechanisms, such as mutexes, to ensure that the work queue can be safely accessed and modified by multiple threads concurrently without causing data corruption or race conditions.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the class to ensure that all member functions are thread-safe and that the work queue operates correctly in a multi-threaded environment.

```cpp
#include <deque>
#include <mutex>

template <typename T>
class ThreadSafeWorkQueue {
private:
    std::deque<T*> _works;
    std::mutex _work_lock;

public:
    void add(T* item) {
        _work_lock.lock();
        _works.push_back(item);
        _work_lock.unlock();
    }

    T* get() {
        _work_lock.lock();
        T* item = _works.front();
        _works.pop_front();
        _work_lock.unlock();
        return item;
    }

    void rest(T* t) {
        _work_lock.lock();
        for (typename std::deque<T*>::iterator iter = _works.begin(); iter != _works.end(); iter++) {
            if (*iter == t) {
                _works.erase(iter);
                break;
            }
        }
        _work_lock.unlock();
    }

    size_t available() const {
        _work_lock.lock();
        size_t count = _works.size();
        _work_lock.unlock();
        return count;
    }
};
```

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

void producer(ThreadSafeWorkQueue<int>& workQueue) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        workQueue.add(new int(i));
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));
    }
}

void consumer(ThreadSafeWorkQueue<int>& workQueue) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        int* item = workQueue.get();
        std::cout << "Consumed: " << *item << std::endl;
        delete item;
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(200));
    }
}

int main() {
    ThreadSafeWorkQueue<int> workQueue;

    std::thread producerThread(producer, std::ref(workQueue));
    std::thread consumerThread(consumer, std::ref(workQueue));

    producerThread.join();
    consumerThread.join();

    std::cout << "Remaining items in the queue: " << workQueue.available() << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In the solution, the `ThreadSafeWorkQueue` class is completed with the necessary member functions and synchronization mechanisms to ensure thread safety. The `add`, `get`, `rest`, and `available` member functions are implemented with appropriate locking and unlocking of the `_work_lock` mutex to ensure that the work queue operations are thread-safe.

Additionally, a simple demonstration of using the `ThreadSafeWorkQueue` class in a multi-threaded environment is provided in the `main` function. The `producer` and `consumer` functions are used to simulate concurrent adding and retrieving of work items from the queue, and the remaining items in the queue are printed at the end to demonstrate the correctness of the thread-safe operations.

