### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a program that processes a list of GitHub repositories and their star counts. The program should be able to perform various operations on the repository data, such as filtering, sorting, and displaying the repositories based on their star counts.

You are given a code snippet that represents a part of the input data format:
```
<gh_stars>1-10
export class Management2 {}
```
The `<gh_stars>` tag indicates the star range for the following repository. In this case, the repository named "Management2" has a star count between 1 and 10.

Your task is to implement a program that can parse and process a list of such repository entries, and perform operations based on the provided star ranges.

Create a function `processRepositories(input: string[]): string[]` that takes in an array of strings representing the repository data and returns an array of strings representing the processed output. The input array will contain strings in the format `<gh_stars>min-max\nrepository_name {}`, where `min` and `max` are the star count range, and `repository_name` is the name of the repository.

Example:
Input:
```
[
  "<gh_stars>1-10\nexport class Management2 {}",
  "<gh_stars>20-30\nexport class ProjectX {}",
  "<gh_stars>5-15\nexport class LibraryY {}"
]
```
Output:
```
[
  "Management2: 1-10 stars",
  "LibraryY: 5-15 stars"
]
The output should contain the names of the repositories along with their respective star ranges, sorted in ascending order of the minimum star count.

### Solution:
```typescript
function processRepositories(input: string[]): string[] {
  const repositories: { name: string, stars: string }[] = [];

  for (const entry of input) {
    const [starsRange, name] = entry.split('\n');
    const [min, max] = starsRange.match(/\d+/g);
    repositories.push({ name: name.trim(), stars: `${min}-${max}` });
  }

  repositories.sort((a, b) => {
    const minA = parseInt(a.stars.split('-')[0]);
    const minB = parseInt(b.stars.split('-')[0]);
    return minA - minB;
  });

  return repositories.map(repo => `${repo.name}: ${repo.stars} stars`);
}

const input = [
  "<gh_stars>1-10\nexport class Management2 {}",
  "<gh_stars>20-30\nexport class ProjectX {}",
  "<gh_stars>5-15\nexport class LibraryY {}"
];
const output = processRepositories(input);
console.log(output); // Output: ["Management2: 1-10 stars", "LibraryY: 5-15 stars"]
```

