                var usingDirectives = lines.Where(line => line.TrimStart().StartsWith("using")).ToList();
                usingDirectives.Sort();

                var uniqueUsingDirectives = usingDirectives.Distinct().ToList();

                for (int i = 0; i < lines.Length; i++)
                {
                    if (lines[i].TrimStart().StartsWith("using"))
                    {
                        lines[i] = uniqueUsingDirectives.First();
                        uniqueUsingDirectives.RemoveAt(0);
                    }
                }

                File.WriteAllLines(file, lines);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, we complete the program by iterating through each C# file, reading its content, sorting the using directives, removing duplicates, and then overwriting the original file with the modified content. We use LINQ to filter and sort the using directives, ensuring that the directives are sorted in ascending order and any duplicates are removed. Finally, we write the modified content back to the original file using `File.WriteAllLines`.

