Your program should take a string representing the HTML code as input and output the maximum depth of nested tags within the code.

For example, given the following HTML code snippet:
```html
<div>
	<p>
		<span></span>
	</p>
</div>
```
The maximum depth of nested tags is 3, as the `<span>` tag is nested within the `<p>` tag, which is nested within the `<div>` tag.

Write a function `maxNestedDepth` that takes a string `htmlCode` as input and returns the maximum depth of nested tags within the HTML code.

Function signature: `int maxNestedDepth(String htmlCode)`

Assume the input HTML code is well-formed and valid, and the tags are properly nested.

### Solution:
```java
public class MaxNestedDepth {
    public static int maxNestedDepth(String htmlCode) {
        int maxDepth = 0;
        int currentDepth = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < htmlCode.length(); i++) {
            if (htmlCode.charAt(i) == '<') {
                if (htmlCode.charAt(i + 1) != '/') {
                    currentDepth++;
                    if (currentDepth > maxDepth) {
                        maxDepth = currentDepth;
                    }
                } else {
                    currentDepth--;
                }
            }
        }

        return maxDepth;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlCode = "<div>\n\t<p>\n\t\t<span></span>\n\t</p>\n</div>";
        System.out.println(maxNestedDepth(htmlCode)); // Output: 3
    }
}
```

The `maxNestedDepth` function iterates through the HTML code and maintains a count of the current depth by tracking the opening and closing tags. The maximum depth encountered is stored and returned as the result.

