You are tasked with implementing a circular buffer data structure using C++. A circular buffer, also known as a ring buffer, is a fixed-size buffer that overwrites old data with new data when the buffer is full. The circular buffer has a pointer to the head and tail of the buffer, and it supports operations such as insertion, deletion, and retrieval of elements in a circular manner.

Your task is to implement the CircularBuffer class using the provided CircularState.hpp file as a starting point. The CircularBuffer class should have the following functionalities:
1. Constructor: Initialize the circular buffer with a given size.
2. Destructor: Free the memory allocated for the circular buffer.
3. Insertion: Add an element to the circular buffer. If the buffer is full, overwrite the oldest element.
4. Deletion: Remove the oldest element from the circular buffer.
5. Retrieval: Get the element at a specific index in the circular buffer.

You should ensure that the circular buffer operates efficiently and handles edge cases such as buffer overflow and underflow.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

template <typename T>
class CircularBuffer {
private:
    T* buffer;
    int size;
    int head;
    int tail;
    bool full;

public:
    CircularBuffer(int bufferSize) : size(bufferSize), head(0), tail(0), full(false) {
        buffer = new T[size];
    }

    ~CircularBuffer() {
        delete[] buffer;
    }

    void insert(T element) {
        buffer[head] = element;
        if (full) {
            tail = (tail + 1) % size;
        }
        head = (head + 1) % size;
        full = (head == tail);
    }

    void removeOldest() {
        if (!full && head == tail) {
            return;  // Buffer is empty
        }
        tail = (tail + 1) % size;
        full = false;
    }

    T retrieve(int index) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Index out of range");
        }
        int pos = (tail + index) % size;
        return buffer[pos];
    }
};
```

### Solution:
```cpp
// Sample usage of the CircularBuffer class
int main() {
    CircularBuffer<int> buffer(5);

    buffer.insert(1);
    buffer.insert(2);
    buffer.insert(3);
    buffer.insert(4);
    buffer.insert(5);

    std::cout << buffer.retrieve(0) << std::endl;  // Output: 1
    std::cout << buffer.retrieve(3) << std::endl;  // Output: 4

    buffer.insert(6);
    buffer.insert(7);

    std::cout << buffer.retrieve(0) << std::endl;  // Output: 3 (overwritten by 6)
    std::cout << buffer.retrieve(4) << std::endl;  // Output: 7

    buffer.removeOldest();

    std::cout << buffer.retrieve(0) << std::endl;  // Output: 4 (after removal of 1)
    return 0;
}
```

